<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBJ8"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:6_Carlton_Gardens,_London,_SW1Y_5AD,_United_Kingdom.JPG" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBKA"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:6_Carlton_Gardens,_London,_SW1Y_5AD,_United_Kingdom.JPG" src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/6_Carlton_Gardens%2C_London%2C_SW1Y_5AD%2C_United_Kingdom.JPG/250px-6_Carlton_Gardens%2C_London%2C_SW1Y_5AD%2C_United_Kingdom.JPG" decoding="async" data-file-width="3072" data-file-height="4608" data-file-type="bitmap" height="375" width="250" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/6_Carlton_Gardens%2C_London%2C_SW1Y_5AD%2C_United_Kingdom.JPG/500px-6_Carlton_Gardens%2C_London%2C_SW1Y_5AD%2C_United_Kingdom.JPG 2x" class="mw-file-element" id="mwBKE"/></a><figcaption id="mwBKI">6 Carlton Gardens, London</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwBKM">BAE Systems has its head office and its <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Registered_office" title="Registered office" id="mwBKQ">registered office</a> in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_of_Westminster" title="City of Westminster" id="mwBKU">City of Westminster</a>, London.<sup about="#mwt588" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-163" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-163"}}'><a href="#cite_note-163" id="mwBKY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBKc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKg">[</span>163<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKk">]</span></span></a></sup><!--Registered office--><sup about="#mwt590" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-164" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-164"}}'><a href="#cite_note-164" id="mwBKo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBKs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKw">[</span>164<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBK0">]</span></span></a></sup><!--Head office--> In addition to its central London offices, it has an office in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Farnborough,_Hampshire" title="Farnborough, Hampshire" id="mwBK4">Farnborough</a>, Hampshire, that houses functional specialists and support functions.<sup about="#mwt591" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-165" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-165"}}'><a href="#cite_note-165" id="mwBK8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBLA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLE">[</span>165<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="15" id="mwBLM" aria-labelledby="Corporate_governance"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Corporate_governance">Corporate governance</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=BAE_Systems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=15" title="Edit section: Corporate governance"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwBLQ">BAE Systems' Chair is <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cressida_Hogg" title="Cressida Hogg" id="mwBLU">Cressida Hogg</a>. As of July 2025, the executive directors are <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Woodburn" title="Charles Woodburn" id="mwBLY">Charles Woodburn</a> (CEO), Brad Greve and Tom Arsenault. The non-executive directors are Crystal E. Ashby, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_Corley" title="Elizabeth Corley" id="mwBLc">Elizabeth Corley</a>, Angus Cockburn, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ewan_Kirk" title="Ewan Kirk" id="mwBLg">Ewan Kirk</a>, Ian Tyler, Nicole Piasecki, Stephen Pearce, Jane Griffiths and Nick Anderson.<sup about="#mwt594" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-BAEboard_166-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"BAEboard"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-BAEboard-166"}}'><a href="#cite_note-BAEboard-166" id="mwBLk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBLo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLs">[</span>166<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBL0">The company's first CEO, John Weston, was forced to resign in 2002 in a boardroom "coup" and was replaced by Mike Turner.<sup about="#mwt597" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Wachman-20020321_167-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Wachman-20020321"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Wachman-20020321-167"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Wachman-20020321-167" id="mwBL4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBL8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMA">[</span>167<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBME">]</span></span></a></sup> <i id="mwBMI">The Business</i> reported that Weston was ousted when non-executive directors informed the chairman that they had lost confidence in him. Further, it was suggested that at least one non-executive director was encouraged to make such a move by the MoD due to the increasingly fractious relationship between BAE Systems and the government.<sup about="#mwt600" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Business_168-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Business"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Business-168"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Business-168" id="mwBMM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBMQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMU">[</span>168<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMY">]</span></span></a></sup> As well as the terms of the Nimrod contract, Weston had fought against the MOD's insistence that one of the first three Type 45 destroyers should be built by VT Group. <i id="mwBMc">The Business</i> said he considered this "competition-policy gone mad".<sup about="#mwt603" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-169" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-169"}}'><a href="#cite_note-169" id="mwBMg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBMk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMo">[</span>169<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMs">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBMw">It is understood that Turner had a poor working relationship with senior MoD officials, for example with former Defence Secretary <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geoff_Hoon" title="Geoff Hoon" id="mwBM0">Geoff Hoon</a>. The first meeting between Hoon and BAE's new chairman Dick Olver in 2004 was said to have gone well; a MoD official commented "He is a man we can do business with".<sup about="#mwt606" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-170" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-170"}}'><a href="#cite_note-170" id="mwBM4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBM8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNA">[</span>170<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNE">]</span></span></a></sup> It has been suggested that relations between Turner and Olver were tense.<sup about="#mwt609" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-171" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-171"}}'><a href="#cite_note-171" id="mwBNI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBNM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNQ">[</span>171<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNU">]</span></span></a></sup> On 16 October 2007, the company announced that Mike Turner would retire in August 2008. <i id="mwBNY">The Times</i> called his departure plans "abrupt" and a "shock", given previous statements that he wished to retire in 2013 at the age of 65.<sup about="#mwt612" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-172" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-172"}}'><a href="#cite_note-172" id="mwBNc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBNg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNk">[</span>172<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNo">]</span></span></a></sup> Despite suggestions that BAE Systems would prefer an American CEO due to the increasing importance of the United States defence market to the company and the opportunity to make a clean break from corruption allegations and investigations related to the Al-Yamamah contracts, the company announced on 27 June 2008, that it had selected the company's chief operating officer, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ian_King_(businessman)" title="Ian King (businessman)" id="mwBNs">Ian King</a>, to succeed Turner with effect from 1 September 2008. The <i id="mwBNw">Financial Times</i> noted that King's career at Marconi distances him from the British Aerospace-led Al Yamamah project.<sup about="#mwt615" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-ftbaesearch_173-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"ftbaesearch"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-ftbaesearch-173"}}'><a href="#cite_note-ftbaesearch-173" id="mwBN0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBN4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBN8">[</span>173<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBOE">Charles Woodburn succeeded Ian King as CEO on 1 July 2017. Woodburn joined BAE Systems in May 2016 as Chief Operating Officer and Executive Board Director following over 20 years' international experience in senior management positions in the oil and gas industry.<sup about="#mwt618" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Ian_King,_Chief_Executive,_BAE_Systems_plc,_has_today_informed_the_Board_that_he_will_retire_on_30_June_2017._174-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Ian King, Chief Executive, BAE Systems plc, has today informed the Board that he will retire on 30 June 2017."},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Ian_King,_Chief_Executive,_BAE_Systems_plc,_has_today_informed_the_Board_that_he_will_retire_on_30_June_2017.-174"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Ian_King,_Chief_Executive,_BAE_Systems_plc,_has_today_informed_the_Board_that_he_will_retire_on_30_June_2017.-174" id="mwBOI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBOM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOQ">[</span>174<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOU">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

